Here’s how you’d wire up the built‑in helper so that it produces a **predicate** (a boolean test) against a `Join` object.  
Just plug your *accepted* values into the parameters – note that the API expects them as strings because it mirrors the original Java method signatures.

```javascript
// Import / expose the generated helper from the plugin module.
// (In practice this would come from whatever build system you’re using.)
import {
  VotingOnlyNodePlugin_fullMasterWithOlderState
} from "./generated/voting-only-node-plugin";

// Create the predicate once, reusing it wherever needed:
const isFullMasterWithOlderState =
  VotingOnlyNodePlugin_fullMasterWithOlderState({
    localAcceptedTerm: "42",      // <-- your local node’s accepted term
    localAcceptedVersion: "7"     // <-- your local node’s accepted version
  });

// Now you can run it against any Join instance:
if (isFullMasterWithOlderState(joinObj)) {
  console.log("This join comes from a full master whose state is older.");
} else {
  console.log("Either not a full master or its state isn’t older.");
}
```

### What the helper actually returns

Under the hood, the generated function builds a closure that evaluates the following logic against each incoming `Join` record:

| Condition | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| `join.isFull()` | Checks whether the joining member claims “full” status. |
| `!join.hasVoted()` | Ensures the member has never voted yet (`hasVoted == false`). |
| `join.getLocalMember().getId() === null` | Confirms there is no existing local ID associated with the join. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getId() !== null && !join.getRemoteMember().isEmpty()` | Verifies the remote side supplies a non‑empty identifier. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getLastSeenTimestampMs() > 0` | Requires a valid timestamp indicating recent activity. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getLastSeenTimestampMs() < Date.now()` | Makes sure the last seen time is still in the past relative to now. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getLastSeenTimestampMs() >= join.getRemoteMember().getCreatedAtMs()` | Guarantees the last seen time is after creation. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getLastSeenTimestampMs() <= join.getRemoteMember().getUpdatedAtMs()` | Ensures the last seen time doesn’t exceed the latest update. |
| `join.getRemoteMember().getLastSeenTimestampMs() + 10000 > Date.now()` | Asserts the last seen event happened within the last ~10 s. |

If all those conditions hold true **and** the remote member’s current term/majority/version pair is strictly less than the supplied `(term, major)` tuple—i.e., the remote node’s state is *older* than our own—the predicate resolves to `true`. Otherwise it yields `false`.

Feel free to drop this predicate straight into any filtering pipeline, stream consumer, or unit test where you want to isolate only those joins coming from a stale full master.

